WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.760
YieldShield uses Chainlink CRE to compare its
public API with actual Monad testnet

2
00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:11.280
state. A cron trigger orchestrates one HTTP
snapshot and same-block

3
00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:16.510
EVM reads. The blockchain reads use the exact
block named by the API,

4
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.010
avoiding comparisons between different
moments.

5
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:25.050
The workflow checks backing, reservations and
paused status for two AUSD pools,

6
00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.400
plus the price and freshness state of three
configured feeds.

7
00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.710
It reports mismatches, stale observations and
other alerts.

8
00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:38.170
Here is the official CRE CLI simulation and
its recorded result.

9
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:44.080
Today’s run completed successfully and
identified two critical alerts: the MON

10
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.090
and staked MON prices are unavailable.

11
00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.190
The pool and API observations agree.

12
00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:56.510
This is read-only orchestration with live HTTP
and EVM capabilities.

13
00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.610
It does not move funds or change settlement
rules.

14
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:06.180
The demonstrated level is a local simulation,
not a continuously deployed DON monitor or

15
00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:07.270
a solvency guarantee.
